  "text_md": "with arbitrary λ and arbitrary coefficients c i . They satisfy\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nand the parameter λ can be chosen such that the test example has the desired regularity.\n\n## 3.2 Boundary conditions\n\n̸\n\nAs in Subsection 2.2, the coefficients c i and the parameter λ can be used to satisfy homogeneous boundary conditions. Two boundary conditions for both θ = 0 and θ = ω give a homogeneous linear system of 4 equations which has a non-trivial solution iff the determinant vanishes. This condition is used to find again a countable number of values of λ . Let us sketch this approach for the case of Dirichlet boundary conditions and λ = 0.\n\nThe condition U (0) = 0 leads to\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nhence\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThe 2 × 2 linear system U ( ω ) = 0 for the coefficients c 3 and c 4 has the determinant\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThis means that for given angle ω one gets the corresponding exponents λ ∈ C by solving (separately) the two transcendental, scalar equations sin λω = ± λ sin ω . All values Re λ ∈ [ 1 2 , 4] are given for ω k = kπ/ 10, k = 4 , 5 , . . . , 20, in [Dau89].\n\n## 3.3 Weak and very weak solutions\n\nAs in Subsection 2.3, the pair ( u, p ) is a weak solution for λ &gt; 0 and a very weak solution for -min(1 , ξ ) &lt; λ ≤ 0, where\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nin the case of Dirichlet boundary conditions. The weak solution ( u, p ) ∈ H 1 (Ω) × L 2 0 (Ω) is defined by\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThe very weak solution ( y, p ) ∈ L 2 (Ω) × P ′ with P = { v ∈ H 1 (Ω) ∩ L 2 0 (Ω) : r -1 v ∈ L 2 (Ω) } is defined by\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nwhere V := { ( v, q ) ∈ H 1 0 (Ω) × L 2 0 (Ω): -∆ v + ∇ q ∈ L 2 (Ω) , ∇· v ∈ P } , see [ALP24].",
